> Air pollution: health impacts
> Health effects and economic costs of environmental stressors, including contaminants released to indoor and outdoor air, water, and soil, are quantified in this report using a framework based on an integrated science assessment. An integrated impact pathway analysis (Figure ‎3.8) combines evidence and methods from multiple natural and social science disciplines, including atmospheric physics and chemistry, epidemiology, toxicology, material science, and economic valuation.
> Air pollution is a complex mixture of substances, comprising carbonaceous material (elemental and organic matter), gaseous pollutants (such as sulphur and nitrogen oxides and greenhouse gases), heavy metals (e.g. lead), primary and secondary inorganic and organic aerosols (e.g. particulates emitted from diesel combustion, biomass burning, or from cooking). Fossil fuel use is the dominant source of greenhouse gas emissions (including methane and other short-lived climate pollutants), as well as many co-emitted air pollutants that degrade ecosystems and contribute to noncommunicable diseases in humans. These include increased cardiovascular and respiratory morbidity and mortality, elevated cancer risk, and adverse effects on infant and child development.
> 
> Figure ‎3.8 Stages in the Impact Pathway health-economic risk analysis. The air pollution impact pathway approach explicitly traces pollutants from their emission into the environment through atmospheric dispersion, chemical transformation during atmospheric aging, and removal by dry and wet deposition. Secondary pollutants may form through chemical reactions, such as ozone produced from precursors including methane, nitrogen oxides and volatile organic compounds. Exposure to these pollutants disproportionally affects vulnerable populations groups such as children, older adults, and individuals with pre-existing illness, who face elevated risks of adverse health outcomes, ranging from mild symptoms to severe disease requiring medical treatment or resulting in premature death (see e.g. WHO 2025a; Forastiere et al. 2024).
> A wide range of health outcomes associated with air-pollution exposure has been identified in the epidemiological literature. These include diseases of the circulatory system (e.g. ischaemic heart disease and stroke), the respiratory system (e.g. bronchitis – part of the group of conditions identified as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and asthma), neoplasms (e.g. lung cancer), metabolic diseases (e.g. diabetes), and neurological/neurodevelopmental disorders (e.g. Alzheimer’s disease and other dementias).
> Health impacts are quantified using concentration-response functions, which describe the statistical relationship between air pollutant exposure and specific health outcomes in exposed populations. For each health endpoint, physical impacts are calculated by multiplying the attributable fraction - the share of the current health burden that could be averted if the health risk were eliminated – by the baseline number of disease cases. Beyond public health impacts, air pollution also affects agricultural productivity, forests, ecosystems, and the built environment, contributing to the degradation of air, water and soil quality, and drives climate forcing. The quantified physical impacts are subsequently monetised.
> Air pollution imposed a substantial global burden of disease through both mortality and morbidity. Recent evidence synthesized for the WHO EMAPEC project (Forastiere et al 2024) has strengthened the evidence base for morbidity impacts of PM2.5, including new incidences of cardiovascular and pulmonary diseases, lung cancer, diabetes and dementia. This assessment applies the concentration-response functions recommended by the WHO for regions with high ambient PM2.5 concentrations to quantify global morbidity attributable to PM2.5 and the health benefits achievable through mitigation. Mortality from long-term PM2.5 exposure is quantified using risk functions for non-communicable diseases and lower respiratory infections (Burnett et al. 2022), while mortality from short-term ozone exposure is quantified using the risk functions from the WHO HRAPIE2 assessment (WHO 2025b).
> This assessment finds that ambient PM2.5 and ozone together currently cause 6.4 million premature deaths annually worldwide, corresponding to approximately 96 million life years lost. Elevated indoor PM2.5 concentrations in households using solid fuels for cooking contributes an additional estimated 2 million premature deaths, nearly 300,000 of which occur in children. Ambient PM2.5 exposure alone is associated annually with an estimated 5.5 million new cases of childhood asthma, 4.2 million cases of acute myocardial infarctions, 3.8 million cases of COPD, 3.3 million cases of diabetes, 2.5 million cases of stroke, 2.0 million cases of dementia, and 0.5 million cases of lung cancer.
> In the absence of additional policy action, population ageing is expected to increase this burden over the coming decades. A substantial share of these impacts can be avoided through the mitigation measures outlined in Chapter 2, including both decarbonization and air pollution controls.
> Population vulnerability is another important determinant of health outcomes. Figure ‎3.9 illustrates the relationship between pollution-related mortality rates and country-level income. It shows a clear inverse relationship with higher income countries experiencing lower mortality rates from air pollution. This pattern is only partly explained by lower pollution levels, which decline markedly only in countries with GDP per capita above $32,000. More broadly, declining mortality with increasing income reflects reduced vulnerability, associated with stronger healthcare systems, improved living conditions, and greater adaptive capacity.
> 
> Figure ‎3.9 Mortality rate from PM2.5 exposure, by country. Points represent countries, with colors according to world regions and sizes proportional to population. Results shown for 2019. Source: GapMinder (https://www.gapminder.org/tools/).
> The global health burden attributable to ambient PM2.5 (Figure ‎3.10) as annual new disease incidences and premature deaths. Owing to demographic change, the total burden increases over time in the Baseline scenario. Disease-specific trends differ: cardiovascular disease and dementia increase with population ageing, while childhood asthma declines. Globally, between 60 and 70 per cent of this burden, depending on health outcomes, can be avoided through full implementation of the measures assessed in this report, indicated by the hatched overlays in Figure ‎3.10.
> 
> Figure ‎3.10 Morbidity and mortality burden from PM2.5: New cases of different diseases, and premature deaths, attributable to PM2.5 globally every year, and the fraction avoidable by the measures discussed in this report indicated as hatched overlay. [O3 related deaths are calculated only until 2050 for the FOD, will be extended to 2100]
